---
// src/pages/devlog.astro — ported from devlog.php + includes/devlog-posts/.
// Each post is an entry in the `posts` array below; newest first.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const posts = [
  {
    date: '2026-07-12',
    title: 'Hover previews: from 172 MB gifs to 0.7 MB videos',
    tags: ['this site', 'ffmpeg', 'performance'],
  },
];

function formatDate(iso) {
  return new Date(iso + 'T00:00:00').toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
    year: 'numeric',
    month: 'long',
    day: 'numeric',
  });
}
---
<Layout title="Dev Log" description="Working notes from CJ Nowacek's pipeline bench: what is being built, what broke, and how it was fixed.">
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 1300px;">
    <h2>Dev Log</h2>
    <div class="about-text" style="max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto 40px auto; text-align: left;">
      <p>
        Working notes from the pipeline bench: what I'm building, what broke, and how I thought
        through it. Short entries, posted as the work happens.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="text-content" style="margin-bottom: 50px;">
      <h3 style="color: var(--header-color); margin-bottom: 4px;">{posts[0].title}</h3>
      <p style="color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 14px; margin-top: 0;">
        {formatDate(posts[0].date)} &middot; {posts[0].tags.join(' / ')}
      </p>
      <p>
        The project cards on this site play a motion preview when you hover them. The original
        implementation swapped in an animated gif, and I never checked what those gifs weighed.
        Today I did: the worst one was 172 MB. Hovering that card on a normal connection would never
        finish loading, which means the feature effectively did not exist for visitors.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three options on the table: re-encode the gifs smaller (still gifs, still huge), animated
        webp (better, but still image-codec economics), or actual video files in a
        <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> tag. Gif as a format is just the wrong tool for photographic
        motion; video codecs exist precisely for this. The only historical reason for hover-gifs is
        autoplay friction, and a muted looping video autoplays fine everywhere now.
      </p>
      <p>
        One ffmpeg pass per clip (h264, crf 28, audio stripped, faststart for instant scrubbing)
        took the full set from roughly 330 MB to 10 MB. The 172 MB gif came out at 0.68 MB, a 250x
        reduction, at visually identical quality for a 4:3 preview tile.
      </p>
      <p>
        The site change was small: the card component renders a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> when a
        project defines one, the same CSS opacity crossfade handles the reveal, and a few lines of
        JS play on hover and pause on leave. The gifs stay as data fallback. The videos are now
        small enough to live in git and deploy with the site instead of being hand-synced like the
        rest of the image assets.
      </p>
      <p>
        Lesson that generalizes: when a feature depends on an asset, the asset's weight is part of
        the feature. Nobody had ever profiled the hover cards, so the heaviest one was silently
        broken. Budget your media like you budget your code.
      </p>
    </article>
    <hr style="max-width: 800px;" />
  </div>
</Layout>
